==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|geminātus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|geminō||to double}}.

===Pronunciation===
; Adjective
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈd͡ʒɛmɪnət/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-geminate (adj).wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
; Verb
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈd͡ʒɛmɪneɪt/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-geminate (verb).wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Forming a [[pair]].
# {{lb|en|phonology|of a [[consonant]]}} [[pronounce|Pronounce]]d [[long]]er and [[consider]]ed as being [[doubled]].
#: {{syn|en|geminated}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=Sara Finley|title=Review of “The Representation and Processing of Compound Words”|url=http://linguistlist.org/issues/19/19-3674.html#1
|passage=For example, Martin (2007) notes that compounds in several languages (including English and Turkish) violate the general phonological principles in the language (e.g., English only allows '''geminate''' consonants in compounds).}}

